WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.200
ˆ

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:56.080
In the lifetime of natural history filmmaking, I've seen many odd animals, but few odder

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:06.280
than these proboscis monkeys in Borneo. I first saw them some 50 years ago. Late one

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:11.820
evening we had a great stroke of luck for a troop of the extraordinary long-nosed proboscis

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:23.920
monkey had come down to the riverbank to feed. When I started filming such creatures, it

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:29.520
was quite easy to show viewers animals that hitherto had only been seen in the wild by

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:43.600
intrepid explorers. As the years passed, one way or another, we got better and better shots

00:01:43.600 --> 00:02:08.400
and in the process I had some memorable encounters. This is a very intelligent animal. On top

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:24.320
of the menu right now is salmon. I think that was pretty clear. I've been lucky enough

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:31.200
to live through what well might be considered the golden age of natural history filmmaking.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:37.320
Almost every year it seemed we found some new way of revealing new things about the

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:47.840
natural world. In the 1950s, much of the wildlife of the planet was still unfilmed, even unknown.

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:53.520
And in the following 60 years, a succession of technical innovations enabled us to reveal

00:02:53.520 --> 00:03:17.840
more and more of the natural world in increasing detail. This is the first natural history

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:43.920
film I ever saw back in 1934 when I was eight and I thought it was wonderful. The man in

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:49.680
the pith helmet is Cherry Kirtan, one of the first people to try and capture the lives

00:03:49.680 --> 00:04:13.360
of wild animals on film. Kirtan travelled around the world filming wild animals that

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:18.840
of course had never been filmed before. His approach was hardly scientific but nonetheless

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:26.440
he was very entertaining. His sister, a typical flapper, not content with being one of the

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:35.800
fair sex, wants to join the air sex but resigns herself to just a flip here, a flap there

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:44.680
and a flap in between. For all its obvious flaws, Kirtan's films captured my childish

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:55.720
imagination and made me dream of travelling to far off places to film wild animals. And

00:04:55.720 --> 00:05:02.120
this is one of the very cameras that Cherry Kirtan used. It's enclosed in a wooden box,

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:14.000
it was driven by hand and it used 35mm film this distance across. Within a few years it

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:20.680
was superseded by improved models like this one which had a metal box and it worked by

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:29.800
clockwork and it had a variety of lenses but it still used hefty 35mm film. Happily however

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:38.280
there were smaller versions available. A camera like this. This used 16mm film which was only

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:45.120
half the size and it was powered by clockwork. But unfortunately the BBC thought cameras

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:51.800
like this were unprofessional and there was a huge row as to whether or not I could be

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:59.360
allowed to take it. But in the end I did and it was with this that I set off to ramble

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:07.400
around the jungles of the world looking for unfilmed animals. My first natural history

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:12.560
series Zoo Quest recorded the progress of animal collecting expeditions arranged for

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:18.360
the London Zoo and brought to the screen places and animals that had never before been seen

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:26.800
on television or in the cinema come to that. One targeted the largest lizard in the world

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:32.040
which lived on the small Indonesian island of Komodo. Few people had heard of it and

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:37.280
in Indonesian no one seemed very sure where the island was. Eventually we set off with

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:43.680
a fisherman who said that he did but after a couple of days at sea I had my doubts.

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:49.120
I said to the captain you haven't been to Komodo before haven't you? And he said Belum

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:54.880
and I didn't know what Belum meant so I had to go down to the cold and find my Indonesian

00:06:54.880 --> 00:07:02.240
dictionary and I looked up Belum and it said not yet. So it was clear he didn't know by

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:08.960
the way. After a week at sea and having survived encounters with coral reefs and whirlpools

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:16.600
we arrived at what I thought must be Komodo. And I remember wading ashore across the coral

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:23.240
lagoon and finding a tiny little village and saying excuse me what's up is this Komodo?

00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:30.020
And they uh yeah it's a Komodo that was okay. The locals recommended that we should use

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:36.880
a dead goat as bait. Once in the bush we began to build a trap using materials gathered from

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:45.880
nearby as I recorded in my journal. This was the dragon trap with a little bait in there

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:52.240
and when the dragon if he went in the front end to trot on there that pulled it down which

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:57.600
then pulled the ring down which released that rod which then pulled down because there was

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:04.000
a lump of rock on the bottom so clunk down it would go. And now all we had to do was

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:12.880
to wait. There was a rustle in the bush and there was the dragon our first sight of this

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:20.320
magnificent monster. To my surprise we were looking out at the trap and I heard a noise

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:26.240
behind me I turned around and there was a dragon that was taken at that particular moment

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:33.000
looking at me straight in the eye from only about a couple of yards away. And I looked

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:37.560
at it and it looked at me and I thought well at least I might take your photograph so that

00:08:37.560 --> 00:08:44.680
was the photograph I took of him. And then he rather wearily heaved himself up and strolled

00:08:44.680 --> 00:08:56.400
around us and went down into the dry river bed where we'd made the trap. And down came

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:02.200
the door hastily we piled boulders on the door so that he couldn't lift it up we had

00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:09.080
got him. Those early films seem pretty ordinary these days but they were nonetheless popular

00:09:09.080 --> 00:09:20.800
because whatever we showed was new to most of our viewers. So in the 1950s we were taking

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:28.120
cameras like this all over the world and then an Austrian biologist and filmmaker decided

00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:34.940
to try and take it underwater. His name was Hans Haas and he developed his own special

00:09:34.940 --> 00:09:45.260
housing to do that. Hans and his wife Lotte were the first to bring the wonders of life

00:09:45.260 --> 00:09:50.680
under the sea to television and their programs were all the more sensational because a few

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:58.240
people at that time had scuba dived. Take care down there are sharks we are right on

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:07.120
the reef's edge. In the 50s sharks of course had a terrible reputation I mean they were

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:15.840
the killers of the sea anybody in water alongside a shark was clearly courting certain death.

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:25.200
And yet here were Hans and Lotte swimming alongside them. The nation was astounded.

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:30.240
The sequence certainly had shock value but perhaps it was also the first step in changing

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:39.640
our perceptions of sharks and like all television it was still shown in black and white. So

00:10:39.640 --> 00:10:47.480
during the Zoo Quest series I had to describe an animal's color in words. This one was among

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:53.400
the most brilliantly colored of all chameleons in the world. His eyeballs are bright rust

00:10:53.400 --> 00:11:04.320
red and his body and legs striped and blotched with a vivid green. But television was changing

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:13.580
fast. In the 1960s the BBC was given a second television network operating on a higher technical

00:11:13.580 --> 00:11:22.120
standard with the specific job of introducing color television and in 1965 I was put in

00:11:22.120 --> 00:11:29.120
charge of it with an office here in the television center in London. To demonstrate that color

00:11:29.120 --> 00:11:34.620
on television could be both accurate and not garish I commissioned a series about the history

00:11:34.620 --> 00:11:43.480
of art. It was called Civilization. I'm standing in the Sistine Chapel and above my head is

00:11:43.480 --> 00:11:49.880
one of the greatest works of man Michelangelo's ceiling. It was presented by Kenneth Clarke

00:11:49.880 --> 00:11:55.800
and became a great success. So we followed it with other series on a similar scale about

00:11:55.800 --> 00:12:01.960
science economics and the history of America. But I knew well that the most dazzlingly colorful

00:12:01.960 --> 00:12:10.360
series would be one about wildlife. After eight years in administration I decided I

00:12:10.360 --> 00:12:18.080
wanted to go back to making programs and I put up a suggestion that we should make 13

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:31.120
hour programs in color tracing the whole history of life on earth. Thanks to the development

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:36.860
of jet air travel we were able to film in 30 countries around the globe and as I traced

00:12:36.860 --> 00:12:41.560
the history of life on the planet I could appear to move from one continent to another

00:12:41.560 --> 00:12:47.560
in the space of a single sequence. The South American rainforest, the richest and the most

00:12:47.560 --> 00:12:54.280
varied assemblage of life in the world. These limestones here in Morocco, macaques live

00:12:54.280 --> 00:13:03.360
in many parts of Japan. Life on earth was shown in a hundred different countries and

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:10.960
seen by perhaps as many as 500 million people. Natural history television was now a global

00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:20.840
phenomenon revealing our wonderful world in color to all. During the series we made

00:13:20.840 --> 00:13:26.760
full use of both color and scuba gear to help show the underwater world as never before.

00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:34.800
I even tried to follow Hans Haas' lead exploring the underwater world. One of the problems

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:40.320
with underwater films of course was that you can't talk underwater. Most of the time

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:45.280
if you've got a breathing apparatus on your back you've got something in your mouth.

00:13:45.280 --> 00:13:50.720
But Alastair, one of my producer colleagues, was very keen that we should try and introduce

00:13:50.720 --> 00:13:55.480
the presenter talking to camera underwater. There was a wonderful new invention called

00:13:55.480 --> 00:14:01.160
the bubble helmet. This is it and you can put a microphone in one side of it. So we

00:14:01.160 --> 00:14:06.760
went down to the swimming pool in the hotel where we were staying and this was screwed

00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:12.520
on my head. It took a long time to screw it down tight, to make it water tight. I put

00:14:12.520 --> 00:14:19.560
it on like this and I waded into the water and I hadn't gone more than about a foot

00:14:19.560 --> 00:14:26.080
underwater when suddenly water started bubbling in. Very alarming, water rising around you

00:14:26.080 --> 00:14:29.880
and I was going to drown. How long would it take me to get this off? So I came out in

00:14:29.880 --> 00:14:36.600
a hurry. There was a fault I said. Nonsense as I would say, give it to me. So he changed

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:41.280
this, put on his shoulders, put it on his head and I with some pleasure screwed it down

00:14:41.280 --> 00:14:47.600
quite tight and he waded into the pool and he came out even quicker with me than water.

00:14:47.600 --> 00:14:54.120
It was gesticulating to get it off and I finally took it off and he said there's a fault.

00:14:54.120 --> 00:15:01.840
I said yes there is. So I happily left the helmet behind and reverted to my old mask

00:15:01.840 --> 00:15:07.920
and scuba gear when it came to my next underwater assignment to reveal the extraordinary social

00:15:07.920 --> 00:15:14.720
behaviour and intelligence of dolphins. They're full of curiosity, they play with odd things

00:15:14.720 --> 00:15:19.120
they find such as twigs and swimming among them leaves you in no doubt that they are

00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:40.280
highly intelligent. They will even mimic you as you spin or hang in the water. Until the

00:15:40.280 --> 00:15:45.880
1980s you could only shoot 10 minutes worth of film underwater before you had to come

00:15:45.880 --> 00:15:50.520
back to the surface, open the underwater housing, take out the camera and put in a new roll

00:15:50.520 --> 00:15:58.960
of film. But then video cameras solved that problem. Video tapes ran for 30 minutes and

00:15:58.960 --> 00:16:05.920
now at last we had the chance of properly recording animal behaviour underwater. In

00:16:05.920 --> 00:16:12.200
addition video cameras were far more sensitive so we could record at much lower light levels

00:16:12.200 --> 00:16:18.840
making artificial lights unnecessary. It was a huge breakthrough for underwater filming

00:16:18.840 --> 00:16:25.760
and crucial to the success of the Blue Planet series. Now it was possible to record for

00:16:25.760 --> 00:16:44.520
the first time marlin hunting. The seas and oceans were full of animals whose extraordinary

00:16:44.520 --> 00:17:00.360
behaviour up till now no one had ever seen and the shots just got better and better.

00:17:00.360 --> 00:17:05.800
Cameramen could now stay underwater long enough to capture every moment of the action and

00:17:05.800 --> 00:17:26.280
be in the right place at the right time for the most dramatic events. So now we can capture

00:17:26.280 --> 00:17:45.880
previously unseen animal behaviour throughout the seas of the world. On land it had until

00:17:45.880 --> 00:17:54.120
now been impossible to film animals behaving naturally at night when most mammals are active.

00:17:54.120 --> 00:18:06.480
All we could do was shine a spotlight on them and film them as they ran away. And it was

00:18:06.480 --> 00:18:14.200
the same problem wherever animals lived in darkness. Caves are fascinating places but

00:18:14.200 --> 00:18:20.440
difficult places to work in. When I first came here to this one in Gomantan in Borneo

00:18:20.440 --> 00:18:26.360
back in 1972 we had to bring a lot of lights with us in order to film the many millions

00:18:26.360 --> 00:18:35.920
of birds and bats that live in here. And the droppings of all those creatures make the

00:18:35.920 --> 00:18:57.240
cave reek of ammonia. The smell brings it all back to me. When I was here 40 years ago

00:18:57.240 --> 00:19:04.880
the director said there's a pile of droppings far in the cave which goes right up the roof.

00:19:04.880 --> 00:19:11.040
Why don't you climb up to the top? When I got to the top he shouted say something. So

00:19:11.040 --> 00:19:25.940
I tried. And what it is is bats packed tight on the roof here. They're flying now all around

00:19:25.940 --> 00:19:36.800
my head. This cave, this particular part of it, makes ammonia is really quite choking,

00:19:36.800 --> 00:19:46.600
makes a very perfect place for a home. One of the really astounding things is that this

00:19:46.600 --> 00:19:52.880
immense number of bats flying around here in a panic, not one of them is colliding with

00:19:52.880 --> 00:19:59.920
the other. Nor indeed am I in any danger whatsoever of being hit by them. And then the director

00:19:59.920 --> 00:20:07.840
said cut. The camera stopped. The lights went out and a bat flew straight in my face. So

00:20:07.840 --> 00:20:15.440
perhaps their much praised echo location is not quite as perfect as people say. The film

00:20:15.440 --> 00:20:22.840
cameras we used then needed normal white light like these. But the problem with that of course

00:20:22.840 --> 00:20:29.840
is that they disturb animals that are accustomed to living in the dark. But then the security

00:20:29.840 --> 00:20:36.920
industry developed a new type of camera like this one which uses infrared light and doesn't

00:20:36.920 --> 00:20:42.840
need these lights but nonetheless can see in the dark as you can see. I turn off one,

00:20:42.840 --> 00:20:53.040
I turn off the other. Now even though it's pitch dark you can see me. Most animals like

00:20:53.040 --> 00:20:58.480
us can't see infrared and that meant that with these cameras we could now watch them

00:20:58.480 --> 00:21:07.000
behaving perfectly normally in the dark. And that revealed some extraordinary behaviour

00:21:07.000 --> 00:21:16.520
and also led to one or two pretty uncomfortable moments. Lions are mostly active at night

00:21:16.520 --> 00:21:25.680
and seldom roar during the day. We tried to persuade them to do so with the help of scientists

00:21:25.680 --> 00:21:52.080
by paying back the roar of a strange lion to a resident pride. Even that didn't work.

00:21:52.080 --> 00:21:58.920
But 12 years later I set off in an open-sided Land Rover with the latest infrared technology

00:21:58.920 --> 00:22:13.800
to try again. As usual they were sleeping. I would have to wait for darkness. We drive

00:22:13.800 --> 00:22:20.560
up, I go on one side, the camera goes on the other and the lion starts roaring. But the

00:22:20.560 --> 00:22:27.320
problem is I can't see where it is. I can't even see where the camera is. Cue, says the

00:22:27.320 --> 00:22:33.480
producer. So I start trying to say my piece, trying not to be too frightened of this lion

00:22:33.480 --> 00:22:37.600
which is somewhere in the blackness as far as I can make out within a couple of yards

00:22:37.600 --> 00:22:42.920
on me and no side on the Land Rover. And I then had to do my piece to camera looking

00:22:42.920 --> 00:22:48.960
around to see where on earth the camera was. And now in the darkness there are a number

00:22:48.960 --> 00:22:57.960
of them roaring just around here. There are two I know within three or four yards of where

00:22:57.960 --> 00:23:05.920
I am now and there's a third perhaps 20 yards over there though it's difficult to tell because

00:23:05.920 --> 00:23:16.720
it's pitch black. Those are not aggressive roars. They are communication roars. But they

00:23:16.720 --> 00:23:34.000
are quite enough to chill the blood in the blackness of the night. A few years later

00:23:34.000 --> 00:23:39.520
similar technology made it possible to film one of the most extraordinary hunting sequences

00:23:39.520 --> 00:24:00.880
ever recorded using whole batteries of infrared lights mounted on vehicles. A solitary lion

00:24:00.880 --> 00:24:12.240
stands no chance but the whole pride is here. There are 30 of them and they are specialist

00:24:12.240 --> 00:24:41.640
elephant hunters. This remarkable behaviour could not have

00:24:41.640 --> 00:24:46.640
been filmed in any other way and it proved conclusively what many had doubted that a

00:24:46.640 --> 00:24:55.520
big pride of lions can indeed bring down and kill an animal as big as an elephant. Other

00:24:55.520 --> 00:25:01.160
cameras were developed that worked simply by concentrating what little light comes from

00:25:01.160 --> 00:25:07.320
the stars and the moon. And we used such a star like camera to record an encounter I

00:25:07.320 --> 00:25:15.040
had with a wonderful New Zealand nocturnal bird, the kiwi. We heard of a place where

00:25:15.040 --> 00:25:21.040
kiwis came out of the bush and walked along the beach looking for sandhoppers. Now they

00:25:21.040 --> 00:25:28.720
find their way by smell so I thought how can I conceal myself. So I lay on the tide line

00:25:28.720 --> 00:25:37.000
where all the rotting seaweed was lying around. I just lay on it and this little enchanting

00:25:37.000 --> 00:25:43.800
little creature came slowly along probing its beak into the sand, blowing out the sand,

00:25:43.800 --> 00:25:51.120
coming closer. Probing sand with your nostrils is all very well but it does clog them up

00:25:51.120 --> 00:26:04.880
and so you need to blow them clear every now and then. Its sense of smell is so acute it

00:26:04.880 --> 00:26:12.360
can pick out the largest juiciest hoppers deep in the sand without even seeing them.

00:26:12.360 --> 00:26:20.440
Our starlight camera can see much better than I can. I need a torch to see this extraordinary

00:26:20.440 --> 00:26:34.320
creature properly but it doesn't seem to mind. He comes right up to me because his eyes are

00:26:34.320 --> 00:26:41.320
very small, poor eyesight putting it mildly but he can smell but he didn't because the

00:26:41.320 --> 00:26:54.120
seaweed was even stronger smelling than me. There are other ways of filming in the dark

00:26:54.120 --> 00:27:03.600
by using thermal cameras like this one. Up above me there are a lot of bats and the camera

00:27:03.600 --> 00:27:12.000
shows them as different colours. The yellow lights here are bats that have just flown

00:27:12.000 --> 00:27:19.240
into the cave and are still warm from their exertion. As well as revealing where animals

00:27:19.240 --> 00:27:26.000
are, the thermal camera can also show something about the condition they're in. For example,

00:27:26.000 --> 00:27:34.760
my face now because I'm rather hot is likely to be an orange colour. When I'm cooler it

00:27:34.760 --> 00:27:41.480
will be red and this probably is verging on the blue but if I take a bottle of cold water

00:27:41.480 --> 00:27:55.960
why that's likely to be black. Very good too. Thermal cameras also proved useful in the

00:27:55.960 --> 00:28:04.520
Galapagos to demonstrate some of the remarkable physiological adaptations of reptiles. Once

00:28:04.520 --> 00:28:09.640
they're thoroughly warmed up marine iguanas can maintain their body temperature just about

00:28:09.640 --> 00:28:16.400
as constantly as I can and what's more at about the same level or indeed slightly higher

00:28:16.400 --> 00:28:24.520
around 37 degrees centigrade. But when they go into the cold sea to feed on submerged

00:28:24.520 --> 00:28:36.120
seaweed their temperature falls very rapidly. A recently emerged iguana is black, it's

00:28:36.120 --> 00:28:45.880
chilled to the bone. Now they need heat in order to be able to digest that meal of seaweed

00:28:45.880 --> 00:28:59.360
and they get that by spreading themselves on these black hot sun baked rocks. So thermal

00:28:59.360 --> 00:29:11.000
cameras reveal just how skilled reptiles are at harnessing the power of the sun. One of

00:29:11.000 --> 00:29:15.080
the things we discovered when we were starting work on Trials of Life was that there was

00:29:15.080 --> 00:29:20.760
a new lens which enabled you to have an object close to the camera, a small little creature

00:29:20.760 --> 00:29:26.440
perhaps, and yet have all the distance right to the far horizon completely in focus so

00:29:26.440 --> 00:29:32.400
that I would be able to walk up from the distance to something that was quite close to camera

00:29:32.400 --> 00:29:38.200
all the time being in focus. It's not always easy to decide in these partnerships which

00:29:38.200 --> 00:29:45.360
is exploiting which. The balance of advantage is often very delicate. Take for example these

00:29:45.360 --> 00:29:52.120
ants in Australia. They are extremely ferocious and normally they'll rip apart any caterpillar

00:29:52.120 --> 00:29:58.280
but see how they're treating this one. When we first saw that shot in the viewing theatre

00:29:58.280 --> 00:30:05.080
we all went down to the canteen and have a cup of tea and talk about it and I heard someone

00:30:05.080 --> 00:30:10.800
next to me who had just joined the team talking to her friend and she said fantastic stuff

00:30:10.800 --> 00:30:16.840
they've got in Australia, amazing, but I would never want to go there myself because you

00:30:16.840 --> 00:30:23.440
know there are caterpillars there that are two feet long. So sometimes with all our optical

00:30:23.440 --> 00:30:33.600
tricks we can get too clever. Nevertheless insects filmed in close up are truly fascinating.

00:30:33.600 --> 00:30:45.000
These are tree ants in Borneo and they have a wonderful way of making their nests. I first

00:30:45.000 --> 00:30:52.120
tried to film how they did so when I was here in Borneo back in the 50s. Then we noticed

00:30:52.120 --> 00:30:58.880
this group with their jaws locked tight in the lower leaf and their hind legs attached

00:30:58.880 --> 00:31:08.440
to the upper leaf. The colony is constructing a new nest and these patient workers are holding

00:31:08.440 --> 00:31:14.640
two leaves of the future nest in position so that other members can fasten them together

00:31:14.640 --> 00:31:22.840
to form the outer wall of their new home. To get those shots we had to tear apart the

00:31:22.840 --> 00:31:29.760
nest to get the ants to work out in the open. These days we can do better than that. This

00:31:29.760 --> 00:31:38.760
is an optical probe that I can make move forward or backwards and even from side to side and

00:31:38.760 --> 00:31:53.440
so with that you can go into the nest and get shots of the ants behaving totally naturally.

00:31:53.440 --> 00:32:02.840
That is a stranger in the nest. That's a little bug which they are attacking. It was technical

00:32:02.840 --> 00:32:11.000
developments like these that allowed us eventually to enter the world of the insect. A motorised

00:32:11.000 --> 00:32:16.640
jib arm enables filmmakers to suspend a camera above a column of aggressive driver ants and

00:32:16.640 --> 00:32:25.080
watch the organised way they hunt through the forest. Workers carry the colony's larvae.

00:32:25.080 --> 00:32:34.080
Horatious soldiers link legs to form a defensive roof and walls enclosing the column. Were

00:32:34.080 --> 00:32:38.760
the camera or the cameraman to accidentally touch just one of these soldiers they would

00:32:38.760 --> 00:32:44.080
all immediately attack but they are blind and they can't see the camera hanging just

00:32:44.080 --> 00:33:01.600
centimetres above them. So we can track along with them as the army takes its prey back

00:33:01.600 --> 00:33:13.880
to the bivouac where the queen is waiting. Wildlife film making can take a lot of patience.

00:33:13.880 --> 00:33:20.840
One may have to spend hours and hours if not days and weeks to film one particular action

00:33:20.840 --> 00:33:27.360
but that can be helped using modern security technology and we used such technology to

00:33:27.360 --> 00:33:36.200
get a shot of something that as far as I know had never been filmed before in the wild.

00:33:36.200 --> 00:33:47.520
Rattlesnakes hunting. Scientists working in New York State had implanted radio transmitters

00:33:47.520 --> 00:33:54.440
in a group of rattlesnakes so that each could be found using an aerial. There is. The camera

00:33:54.440 --> 00:34:02.320
crew placed remotely controlled cameras and infrared lights next to a snake lying in ambush.

00:34:02.320 --> 00:34:07.040
The cameras were attached to motion detectors that would turn them on if anything moved

00:34:07.040 --> 00:34:25.040
in their field of vision. The following night I checked the replay. There's a mouse. It's

00:34:25.040 --> 00:34:32.080
pitch dark and the mouse clearly has no idea that the snake is there but the snake is well

00:34:32.080 --> 00:34:48.000
aware of the mouse. He's worked out that that is the path along which the mice run. Oh my

00:34:48.000 --> 00:35:02.640
goodness. That's a dead mouse. So it was that technology designed to keep burglars out of

00:35:02.640 --> 00:35:15.600
our homes enabled us to record the rattlesnakes hunting strategy in the wild. Another revelatory

00:35:15.600 --> 00:35:22.520
film technique involves playing with time, slowing down the action. Cameraman have longed

00:35:22.520 --> 00:35:29.480
on that simply by increasing the number of images taken per second. Kestrels are known

00:35:29.480 --> 00:35:37.320
as wind hovers because of their apparent ability to hang motionless in the air. And slow motion

00:35:37.320 --> 00:35:42.440
photography enables us to see details of their flying technique that we can't see with the

00:35:42.440 --> 00:35:49.960
naked eye. By filming this trained bird with this special camera we can slow down the motion

00:35:49.960 --> 00:35:56.800
and see exactly how they do it. It's flying at the same speed as the oncoming wind and

00:35:56.800 --> 00:36:05.000
the air flowing over its wings provides just enough lift to keep it airborne. By flying

00:36:05.000 --> 00:36:10.580
as slowly as this they risk stalling because the wind flow over the wing doesn't provide

00:36:10.580 --> 00:36:17.960
enough lift. Slowing down the action by 10 times we can see how the Kestrel extends the

00:36:17.960 --> 00:36:23.000
finger like projection on the leading edge of its wing and spreads its tail feathers

00:36:23.000 --> 00:36:31.040
to generate more lift. Commercial airliners do the same thing when they adjust their wing

00:36:31.040 --> 00:36:39.800
flaps to slow them down for landing. If a Kestrel is to see its prey successfully while

00:36:39.800 --> 00:36:45.840
hovering it has to keep its head perfectly still. Not easy when the wind is constantly

00:36:45.840 --> 00:36:53.000
trying to blow you off position. But in slow motion you can see how the Kestrel responds

00:36:53.000 --> 00:36:59.080
immediately to changes in the wind constantly adjusting the set of its wings and allowing

00:36:59.080 --> 00:37:07.160
its neck to stretch and contract. So that while its body is constantly moving its eyes

00:37:07.160 --> 00:37:17.480
stay fixed and can spot the slightest movement on the ground below. One of my favourite slow

00:37:17.480 --> 00:37:25.640
motion moments was when I was able to fool a lovesick hover fly with a pea shooter.

00:37:25.640 --> 00:37:34.680
It might seem that he's absolutely motionless but in fact he's having to make continual

00:37:34.680 --> 00:37:41.240
changes to adjust for slight currents in the air. It's an amazing piece of acrobatics

00:37:41.240 --> 00:37:50.320
far better than anything that we could do in a helicopter and it's all done in order

00:37:50.320 --> 00:38:00.480
to impress the female to show her that he is superb at holding his territory. With his

00:38:00.480 --> 00:38:06.560
superb eyesight he's ready to spot anything that might whiz by him at high speed that

00:38:06.560 --> 00:38:20.560
could be a female and I might just be able to fool him with a pea shooter. By watching

00:38:20.560 --> 00:38:27.480
his response slow down by about 50 times it's clear that the male is indeed so hyped up

00:38:27.480 --> 00:38:32.280
that he will pursue any fast moving object that comes near him in the hope that it might

00:38:32.280 --> 00:38:42.820
be a female. Those poor males must have been exhausted by the time I'd finished with them.

00:38:42.820 --> 00:38:48.560
By combining the best macro lenses with digital slow motion cameras we were able to reveal

00:38:48.560 --> 00:39:00.120
the extreme athletic prowess of some even tinier creatures. These springtails as their

00:39:00.120 --> 00:39:13.800
name suggests have a rather novel way of jumping. They have a tiny two pronged lever beneath

00:39:13.800 --> 00:39:20.320
their abdomen. One small flick from it can catapult them six inches some 15 centimetres

00:39:20.320 --> 00:39:36.000
into the air. It's the equivalent of a human being jumping over the Eiffel Tower. So with

00:39:36.000 --> 00:39:42.000
slow motion cameras we can watch actions and distinguish details that are impossible to

00:39:42.000 --> 00:40:10.560
see with the naked eye.

00:40:10.560 --> 00:40:18.720
At the other end of the scale we can manipulate time to speed up excessively slow action.

00:40:18.720 --> 00:40:25.480
This is a time-lapse studio where you can control lights and cameras very precisely.

00:40:25.480 --> 00:40:31.840
A film camera shoots 25 frames per second but if you modify one so that it only shoots

00:40:31.840 --> 00:40:37.260
one frame per second and then show the film at normal speed well then you increase the

00:40:37.260 --> 00:40:46.320
speed of action by 25 times. And as the sophistication of time-lapse photography has increased so

00:40:46.320 --> 00:40:52.640
we've been able to show that plants can be as competitive and as aggressive as many an

00:40:52.640 --> 00:41:05.680
animal. And it was the mastery of time-lapse that allowed us to make a series called the

00:41:05.680 --> 00:41:17.280
Private Life of Plants.

00:41:17.280 --> 00:41:22.600
Condensed three months into 20 seconds and the desolation of winter quickly warms into

00:41:22.600 --> 00:41:31.120
the riot of spring.

00:41:31.120 --> 00:41:35.640
You need a week into a minute and you can sense the urgency with which the ground living

00:41:35.640 --> 00:41:48.400
plants race to unfurl their flowers.

00:41:48.400 --> 00:41:55.800
Of all the woodland plants the humble bramble is one of the most aggressive. It waves its

00:41:55.800 --> 00:42:08.840
shoots agitatedly from side to side as if feeling for the best way forward.

00:42:08.840 --> 00:42:14.440
The invading stems, backward pointing spines give it the grip it needs to climb over almost

00:42:14.440 --> 00:42:24.720
anything that stands in its way. It can advance as much as seven centimeters in a day.

00:42:24.720 --> 00:42:30.600
Now digital cameras allow us to see how a shot is developing while we're still taking

00:42:30.600 --> 00:42:37.560
it instead of having to wait till it was finished as we used to have to do with film cameras.

00:42:37.560 --> 00:42:44.720
And we can also use computers attached to small motors to move a camera in between exposed

00:42:44.720 --> 00:43:11.000
frames so that the camera can in fact travel alongside a plant.

00:43:11.000 --> 00:43:17.320
Using this new technology it became possible to condense the arrival of spring in a woodland

00:43:17.320 --> 00:43:31.000
into a few seconds.

00:43:31.000 --> 00:43:36.160
But the wonderful thing about wildlife filmmaking is that no matter how much you've seen and

00:43:36.160 --> 00:43:42.600
filmed there's always going to be something to surprise you. I remember back in 1994 we

00:43:42.600 --> 00:43:49.200
were filming Nepenthes raja, the largest pitcher plant in the world growing up in the mountains

00:43:49.200 --> 00:43:59.400
of Borneo and I made an assumption about how it obtained its nitrogen fertilizer.

00:43:59.400 --> 00:44:11.280
I guess this one contains two or three pints of liquid. It's so big that it catches not

00:44:11.280 --> 00:44:18.300
just insects but even small rodents and one was recorded that had in it the body of a

00:44:18.300 --> 00:44:25.240
drowned rat. So if ever there was a carnivore among plants this is it.

00:44:25.240 --> 00:44:33.440
And I was wrong. In 2010 scientists discovered that the plant gets its nitrogen in a quite

00:44:33.440 --> 00:44:39.560
different way and we couldn't resist going back to see if we could find out what the

00:44:39.560 --> 00:44:40.560
truth was.

00:44:40.560 --> 00:44:48.740
Mount Kinabalu in Sabah is home to many raja pitcher plants. They certainly seem to attract

00:44:48.740 --> 00:44:54.680
insects that fall into their bowls just as other pitchers do. But they also have larger

00:44:54.680 --> 00:45:07.680
visitors. A tree shrew. It's licking the underside of the lid where the pitcher secretes

00:45:07.680 --> 00:45:16.600
nectar with which it lures visitors. But even though its backside is hanging over the bowl

00:45:16.600 --> 00:45:24.080
it doesn't seem to be in any danger of falling in and drowning. So what's going on? It leaves

00:45:24.080 --> 00:45:29.440
a clue. A dropping.

00:45:29.440 --> 00:45:36.200
So the pitcher is a tree shrew toilet. The tree shrew feeds by licking the secretions

00:45:36.200 --> 00:45:42.720
from the pitcher plant's lid and the pitcher plant gets its fertilizer by collecting the

00:45:42.720 --> 00:45:43.720
tree shrew's droppings.

00:45:43.720 --> 00:45:54.320
Wildlife cameraman are always trying to film some piece of animal behavior that no one

00:45:54.320 --> 00:46:03.240
has ever seen before. An aerial photography enables them to do just that.

00:46:03.240 --> 00:46:08.040
In the early days we occasionally managed to get up in a small plane to get a shot of

00:46:08.040 --> 00:46:14.520
the landscape. But the plane vibrated so much that you couldn't use long lenses to get close

00:46:14.520 --> 00:46:23.440
ups of animals and if you went low the roar of the engine frightened them. So we tried

00:46:23.440 --> 00:46:32.760
other forms of aerial transport. Balloons were a little quieter but they took you where the

00:46:32.760 --> 00:46:38.200
wind blew them, not where you wanted to go and getting steady shots was still difficult.

00:46:38.200 --> 00:46:53.200
It wasn't until the invention of a kind of mount that could hold the camera almost miraculously

00:46:53.200 --> 00:46:59.200
free of vibration that it was possible to use the long lenses necessary in order to

00:46:59.200 --> 00:47:05.960
film animals from a height when they didn't even know you were there.

00:47:05.960 --> 00:47:11.120
It's almost impossible to follow a wild dog hunt at ground level through the treacherous

00:47:11.120 --> 00:47:19.200
swamp lands of the Okavango Delta in Africa. But the Planet Earth series used a helicopter

00:47:19.200 --> 00:47:25.040
with a new stabilizing mount that kept the camera vibration free and you could get close

00:47:25.040 --> 00:47:39.120
ups from so high up that the animals below didn't know you were there.

00:47:39.120 --> 00:47:44.440
By intercutting aerial shots and shots from the ground we could show how the dogs worked

00:47:44.440 --> 00:47:54.440
as a team with fresh animals joining the hunt to harry their prey and cut off its escape.

00:47:54.440 --> 00:47:59.700
This new perspective gives us the big picture helping us to understand behavior we could

00:47:59.700 --> 00:48:02.600
only see fragments of before.

00:48:02.600 --> 00:48:32.080
So now we have the techniques to film almost

00:48:32.080 --> 00:48:38.120
anything on land or in the sea or in the air. But to get pictures of animals that lived

00:48:38.120 --> 00:48:41.520
in the past you have to recreate life.

00:48:41.520 --> 00:48:48.680
In the early days our attempts were pretty crude. We used solid models of extinct fish

00:48:48.680 --> 00:48:57.780
placed in swamps to show the arrival of amphibians on land. We moved on to line drawings of dinosaurs

00:48:57.780 --> 00:49:00.560
and I even appeared alongside one.

00:49:00.560 --> 00:49:07.520
It's easy to imagine some 12 foot species of Pelicasaur like a Dimetrodon lying basking

00:49:07.520 --> 00:49:11.880
on the rocks in the early morning sun.

00:49:11.880 --> 00:49:16.800
And then we began to animate the drawings but not very realistically. It would take

00:49:16.800 --> 00:49:22.800
the advent of computer animation to make them move like real animals.

00:49:22.800 --> 00:49:29.560
We wanted to use these new computer techniques to bring to life a mower, the giant extinct

00:49:29.560 --> 00:49:33.000
ostrich like bird of New Zealand.

00:49:33.000 --> 00:49:41.580
First of all I had to walk into a woodland glade holding a mower bone. Then what would

00:49:41.580 --> 00:49:47.560
happen would be that that bone would be suspended. I had to take my hands away and all the rest

00:49:47.560 --> 00:49:52.160
of the bones in the skeleton would appear from nowhere materialized to form the complete

00:49:52.160 --> 00:49:57.280
skeleton. So I had to walk in, hold the bone, then actually take my hands away and let it

00:49:57.280 --> 00:50:01.480
drop which seemed a silly thing to do.

00:50:01.480 --> 00:50:06.480
But electronic trickery made it stay there and then added the rest of the bones of the

00:50:06.480 --> 00:50:09.360
mower skeleton.

00:50:09.360 --> 00:50:22.560
It had just three toes. Its pelvis and its spine lead up to an extraordinarily long neck.

00:50:22.560 --> 00:50:27.600
This bird stood over six feet, two meters tall.

00:50:27.600 --> 00:50:33.560
But then we wanted it to walk away. And so what the computer expert got us to do was

00:50:33.560 --> 00:50:38.320
to imagine where it was going to stand and then conceal ourselves in the vegetation,

00:50:38.320 --> 00:50:45.080
each of us holding a bit of fishing line attached to a branch. And with our computer expert

00:50:45.080 --> 00:50:50.480
conducting us as though he was conducting orchestra, the mower came in, this branch

00:50:50.480 --> 00:50:56.600
was brushed away, and then it reached up and pecked another leaf and the leaf moved and

00:50:56.600 --> 00:51:02.780
then it moved away and the bushes moved. It was really quite convincing.

00:51:02.780 --> 00:51:09.520
The first human settlers on these islands saw these giants alive and called them mowers.

00:51:09.520 --> 00:51:16.800
Among them were the tallest birds that ever existed that weighed over 200 kilos, 400

00:51:16.800 --> 00:51:18.740
pounds.

00:51:18.740 --> 00:51:25.840
So now we could recreate extinct creatures whenever we liked in their entire full color

00:51:25.840 --> 00:51:37.200
animated glory.

00:51:37.200 --> 00:51:42.520
The succession of technological advances has certainly changed the way we make natural

00:51:42.520 --> 00:51:47.500
history films.

00:51:47.500 --> 00:51:53.160
These days with every year that passes, we seem to get more and more equipment, longer

00:51:53.160 --> 00:52:01.200
lenses, more electronic bits of kit. But in the end, often the most memorable shot comes

00:52:01.200 --> 00:52:12.200
from just one camera and one person with a deep understanding of the natural world.

00:52:12.200 --> 00:52:25.240
The film A Wild Snow Leopard was once the ultimate challenge for a wildlife cameraman.

00:52:25.240 --> 00:52:30.880
Doug Allen went to the Himalayas to attempt to do what so many cameramen before him had

00:52:30.880 --> 00:52:32.360
tried but failed.

00:52:32.360 --> 00:52:40.680
I guess this is where you could see it really starts. We're up here in snow leopard country.

00:52:40.680 --> 00:52:49.040
You look around and anywhere, anytime, you might just see it. These are big, big mountains

00:52:49.040 --> 00:52:52.440
and there are not many snow leopards.

00:52:52.440 --> 00:53:01.840
Nevertheless, Doug took to his hide and waited.

00:53:01.840 --> 00:53:09.600
This is tedious stuff. Not a sign.

00:53:09.600 --> 00:53:15.600
If you got just a little bit of a hint, a wee bit of a sighting, now and again your

00:53:15.600 --> 00:53:23.080
spirits would be lifted. But right now, I'd swap a little bit of this animal's charisma

00:53:23.080 --> 00:53:25.960
for a little bit more visibility.

00:53:25.960 --> 00:53:29.760
And things didn't improve even after two weeks.

00:53:29.760 --> 00:53:45.680
Yeah, of course it's boring. It's boring as hell.

00:53:45.680 --> 00:53:51.760
After seven weeks of patiently sitting and watching, these distant shots are all Doug

00:53:51.760 --> 00:53:58.160
managed to film. So he had to return home empty handed.

00:53:58.160 --> 00:54:03.720
The following winter, cameraman Mark Smith took up the challenge and tried a different

00:54:03.720 --> 00:54:07.000
location, this time in Pakistan.

00:54:07.000 --> 00:54:13.920
We just got a lot of snow and we'll be able to track the snow leopard. So we'll have a

00:54:13.920 --> 00:54:19.700
lot of better chance of filming it. So it's just fantastic.

00:54:19.700 --> 00:54:27.560
After that promising start, things didn't go so well for Mark. He and the crew spent

00:54:27.560 --> 00:54:33.420
a fruitless month trudging through the snow.

00:54:33.420 --> 00:54:39.320
Mark spent all Christmas in the mountains with no sign of a snow leopard, but it was

00:54:39.320 --> 00:54:42.200
a much happier new year.

00:54:42.200 --> 00:54:50.720
We just got a report that there's a snow leopard up on the ridge. We were too low where we

00:54:50.720 --> 00:54:55.360
were before. Just trying to get some height to get a better view of it.

00:54:55.360 --> 00:54:58.640
Finally, Mark was rewarded with his first ever glimpse.

00:54:58.640 --> 00:55:06.240
I looked up onto the ridge and I could see this leopard shaped rock, which I'd seen a

00:55:06.240 --> 00:55:12.840
million times before. And I looked through binoculars and it was a leopard just sat there.

00:55:12.840 --> 00:55:17.520
It was perched like just on the top of the rock and it looked down at us and it sort

00:55:17.520 --> 00:55:22.240
of sat down in a sort of sphinx like posture.

00:55:22.240 --> 00:55:31.040
A few days later, Mark's patience paid off. There was not just an adult female, but with

00:55:31.040 --> 00:55:45.000
her a one year old cub.

00:55:45.000 --> 00:55:53.200
Overall, Mark spent eight months in Pakistan and his dedication enabled him to document

00:55:53.200 --> 00:56:04.000
the most intimate moments of a snow leopard's life, including a hunt.

00:56:04.000 --> 00:56:23.000
Silently, she positions herself above her prey.

00:56:23.000 --> 00:56:39.600
Inwan!

00:56:39.600 --> 00:57:06.020
The revelations brought by wildlife films today were beyond my imagination when I set

00:57:06.020 --> 00:57:24.440
out 60 years ago. They have transformed not only our understanding of the natural world,

00:57:24.440 --> 00:57:39.400
but our attitudes towards it. There have been a lot of changes in the way that we have filmed

00:57:39.400 --> 00:57:45.440
the natural world during the last 50, 60 years, but there have also been a great change in the

00:57:45.440 --> 00:57:54.480
way we understand that world and that's what I'll be looking at in the next program.
